use dacc_core::{NonEmptyStr, nonempty_str};

dacc_knowledge::review!("v0013",
    title: NonEmptyStr::new("The tree read by a hand that wrote none of it: what is claimed against what runs"),
    angle: NonEmptyStr::new("A sceptical reading of the architecture, the registry, the gate and the test suite by an agent that wrote none of this code, checking each claim by reading the code it is about rather than the prose that states it, and ranking what does not hold."),
    findings: nonempty_str![
        "major — TESTING.md names two test layers as running every commit that the tree does not contain: 'Provider, recorded — one real cloud API, replayed' and 'Executor — one container per supported distribution' (docs/TESTING.md:25-27), and the prose repeats it ('recorded once against the live account, replayed in CI', docs/TESTING.md:92-94). There is no cloud provider crate, no HTTP client in any manifest, no container harness in any test directory, and .github/workflows/ci.yml runs only cargo xtask ci and the nightly mutants job. What runs instead is the local path — the os provider against a scratch directory through the local executor (crates/nerpa-provider-os/tests/converge.rs). Why it matters: a reader plans trust from a test pyramid that is not there, in a document that already records this exact failure ('this document claimed a harness did it, no harness ever did', docs/TESTING.md:52-55). What to do: rewrite the table to the layers that run, or build the missing layers before the next stage exit; decision 0015 stays immutable, TESTING.md is the claim that moved.",
        "major — INV-COMPAT-002 names an enforcement nobody runs. The entry says a plan change 'fails until the golden is updated and a changelog entry is written' and names 'a change to the plan output ... with no changelog entry' as the bypass its adversarial tests attack (doc/invariant/i0020.rs:7,11). The corpus test fails only until the golden is updated (crates/nerpa-core/tests/plan_stable.rs:79-82) and nothing anywhere reads CHANGELOG.md — no gate in xtask/, no test. The named bypass is half attacked, which is the failure RULES.md:42-49 calls this project's most exposed: an entry marked enforced whose test does not attempt the bypass the entry names, in the registry everything else trusts. What to do: a gate refusing a change to crates/nerpa-core/tests/corpus/ without a CHANGELOG.md hunk in the same commit, or a bypass restated to the half that is attacked, with the changelog named as convention.",
        "major — a refusal that names what moved can name nothing. what_moved diffs the actor and the changes address by address (crates/nerpa-cli/src/approval.rs:183-220), but the number it explains a difference of covers the stages, the migrations and the merges as well (crates/nerpa-core/src/plan.rs:1024-1038). Two plans whose numbers differ only there — a moved() or a merge mark added between the signature and the apply — are refused with 'the world moved between the signature and this run — ' and nothing after the dash (crates/nerpa-cli/src/approval.rs:159-167). The refusal cannot be talked out of and fires in the safe direction; what fails is the promise INV-TRUST-002 and the stage-4 outcome make, that the refusal names what moved. What to do: diff migrations, merges and stage order inside what_moved, or fall back to naming both numbers when the diff comes out empty.",
        "major — nothing in the tree can produce the stage-4 exit evidence. The thrust's outcome is a run on the live fleet where one party applies and another signs, a signature taken before an out-of-band change is refused naming what moved, and the log verifies from the first entry (doc/thrust/t-stage-4-access.rs:6). cargo xtask live runs plan, apply and plan again and stops there (xtask/src/live.rs:176-218): no sign verb, no second actor, no --approval, no journal verify — the four things the outcome is made of are exactly what the harness does not drive. The features themselves are tested with care at the unit level (crates/nerpa-cli/tests/approval.rs:284-328 watches the refusal name the file that moved), but closing the thrust on those alone repeats the pattern of the first finding. What to do: teach the live harness to drive plan --as, sign --as, an out-of-band change, apply --approval and journal verify, and record the run. The run itself needs ssh reach to the real fleet — BatchMode keys to the machines of stage 1 (Rocky and Gentoo behind the Teleport ssh_config, Alpine behind a bastion), sudo -n with a root-owned executor path, two signing keys and an allowed_signers file under [approval] — which is what makes it a stage exit rather than a test.",
        "minor — the actor's scope and expiry do nothing. --as-scope and --as-expiry are read (crates/nerpa-cli/src/main.rs:73-85), carried into the plan and its number (crates/nerpa-core/src/plan.rs:1045-1055), and then never read again: nothing checks a scope and nothing refuses an identity whose expiry passed yesterday (crates/nerpa-core/src/actor.rs:12-13 says so). INV-TRUST-001 names this as the grant's future work and claims nothing else, so the gap is declared; the flags still read like a control, and an operator choosing a one-hour identity has one in name only. What to do: refuse an expiry already past where the actor is made — one comparison — and leave the scope until grants exist.",
        "minor — rule 7's third sentence has no executor. RULES.md:75-81 calls reaching for unwrap_or_default() 'worse than either', but the two checks its own table names see only .unwrap(): the repository rule (xtask/src/lint.rs:170-185) and clippy's unwrap_used (Cargo.toml:151), and the repository rule's test enshrines leaving unwrap_or_default alone (xtask/src/lint.rs:244-248). The pattern sits in library code where a wrong answer is possible — the plan digest's Display truncates through it (crates/nerpa-core/src/plan.rs:964). This is the rules file's own thesis turned on itself: a prohibition whose named holder cannot hold it. What to do: put the pattern in the lint, or mark the sentence as advice so rule and table agree.",
        "minor — the drift class inside the file that polices drift. TESTING.md's 'Running it' block says cargo mutants (docs/TESTING.md:160) while the same file's mutation section and the gate's single entry point say cargo xtask mutants (docs/TESTING.md:111, xtask/src/main.rs:87 — whose header says a second way to run a check never stays in step). One word, in the document whose subject is word-versus-code drift. What to do: fix the line.",
        "nit — the live harness reads convergence by substring and installs through a fixed path. The second plan counts as empty when its output merely contains 'nothing to do' (xtask/src/live.rs:213), and the executor is installed through /tmp/nerpa-executor.install — written by one identity and consumed by root (xtask/src/live.rs:136-139) — a predictable name in a shared /tmp. Operator tooling against machines the operator owns, so the exposure is small; the likelier harm is a wording change passing silently. What to do: match the line whole, and let the far side name its own temporary file.",
        "nit — the unwrap rule stops at a line of text. The repository rules stop at the first literal #[cfg(test)] line (xtask/src/lint.rs:193-197), so production code below an inline test module escapes rule 7 and the module-docs rules; the test at xtask/src/lint.rs:257-278 keeps it that way on purpose. Convention is what keeps it safe today. What to do: nothing urgent; a trailing section of real code is one refactor away from being unchecked.",
        "strength — what held up under sceptical reading: the registry names its own limits where a weaker one would not. INV-REACH-002 admits its one on-purpose violation inside the entry (doc/invariant/i0026.rs:6); INV-LOG-001 and the journal module spell out that truncating the tail still verifies (crates/nerpa-cli/src/journal.rs:18-23); INV-TRUST-002 refuses to claim the signer is who they say they are (doc/invariant/i0065.rs:12); RULES.md keeps its dashes honest (RULES.md:101-110). The claims this review could check held: docs/COMMITS.md's count of the first twenty-five subjects over 72 characters is six, as written; the adversarial tests read assert what the refusal says, not only that it fired (crates/nerpa-cli/tests/approval.rs:319-324); the plan number's encoding carries no catch-all arm and says why (crates/nerpa-core/src/plan.rs:1057-1064); and the hash chain is the chain the module claims (crates/nerpa-cli/src/journal.rs:56-64).",
        "verdict — no blocker was found, and the engine is better argued and better tested than most code that reaches machines as root. Where the project is worst is the seam this reading kept finding: the prose and the registry claiming more than the suite runs (findings 1, 2 and 4). Each of the four majors closes with a gate rather than with a promise, which is the only currency this project itself accepts.",
    ],
    authors: nonempty_str!["An independent reader — an agent that wrote none of this code"],
    decided_at: dacc_core::date!(2026, 10, 4),
);
